WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:14.503
This video shows imperative control. Your code calls the player directly, one command at a time, through the API ref property. Some things, like speed, work only this way.

2
00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:18.899
First, the player. It is one package from npm.

3
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:26.394
That adds the player to the project.

4
00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:31.317
First, we need a handle to the player. Let's create a ref for it.

5
00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:47.903
The ref starts empty, and it holds the player once the player is on the page. Its type is the player's API, imported next to the component.

6
00:00:49.133 --> 00:00:52.999
Now let's add the player in a card, and pass it that ref.

7
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:11.537
The API ref property fills the ref with the player. There is no autoplay here, so nothing plays until we call it.

8
00:01:12.767 --> 00:01:18.017
Now the commands. Let's add a Play button and a Pause button, each calling the player.

9
00:01:28.767 --> 00:01:32.345
Each button calls one method on the player, play or pause.

10
00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:36.257
Let's press Play, and then Pause.

11
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:45.450
It plays, and then stops right where it was, until the next call.

12
00:01:46.667 --> 00:01:52.361
To jump back to the beginning, we set the time to zero. Let's add a Restart button.

13
00:02:03.767 --> 00:02:08.312
Set current time takes milliseconds, so zero is the very start.

14
00:02:09.533 --> 00:02:13.217
Let's play it, pause it on the floor, and then press Restart.

15
00:02:17.267 --> 00:02:21.551
It jumps back to the start, up in the air, and waits there for the next call.

16
00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.894
To show any moment, let's add a slider that sets the player's progress.

17
00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.885
Set current progress takes a number from zero to one, and the player jumps there.

18
00:02:45.133 --> 00:02:46.126
Let's drag it.

19
00:02:51.433 --> 00:02:55.326
Each move of the slider is one call, and the ball follows it.

20
00:02:56.567 --> 00:03:02.810
Speed is a command too. Let's add a button for double speed, and one that plays backwards.

21
00:03:18.067 --> 00:03:25.512
Set playback rate changes the speed. Two is twice as fast, and minus one plays in reverse.

22
00:03:26.733 --> 00:03:34.675
Let's press Play, then double speed, then Reverse, and finally Pause. Watch the spin.

23
00:03:40.867 --> 00:03:49.252
Twice as fast, and then backwards, spinning the other way. Each button is one call, and the player does exactly that.

24
00:03:50.500 --> 00:04:02.856
So, with the API ref property, your code commands the player directly. Play, pause, jump to any moment, and change the speed, each with a single call.
